fact WorkItem(id: "WI-940", description: some(value: "scaland Bootstrap emits an eponymous single-constructor sort as 'enum X: case X(...)' + a separate 'XOps' trait -- the nested X.X that WI-926 forbids.\n\nMEASURED (2026-08-02, delivering WI-935). scaland Bootstrap.generate on the new stdlib/anthill/geometry.anthill emits, for 'sort Vec3 { entity Vec3(x,y,z); operation vec_add(...) = ... ... }':\n\n  package anthill.geometry\n\n  enum Vec3:\n    case Vec3(x: Double, y: Double, z: Double)\n\n  trait Vec3Ops:\n    def vecAdd(a: Vec3, b: Vec3): Vec3\n    def vecSub(a: Vec3, b: Vec3): Vec3\n    def vecScale(c: Double, v: Vec3): Vec3\n    def vecZero(): Vec3\n\nTwo defects in one output. (1) 'enum Vec3: case Vec3(...)' is exactly the nested 'Vec3.Vec3' that docs/kernel-language.md 6.3 / WI-926 rule out -- an eponymous constructor IS its sort, ONE symbol, and the correct emission is a single 'case class Vec3(x: Double, y: Double, z: Double)'. (2) the members are split into a SEPARATE 'Vec3Ops' trait, so the type a consumer holds and the operations on it are two declarations rather than one.\n\nTHIS IS THE SAME DEFECT WI-931 FIXED ON THE RUST SIDE, in the other backend. cpp-gen emitted 'struct Vec3' TWICE for this shape (a C++ redefinition error) because classify_namespace built a data band from the entity-field registry and a traits band from OperationInfo parents, and one symbol satisfied both. The fix was at classify_namespace (the three bands are DISJOINT by contract) plus emit_entity_struct_by_symbol (one symbol, one struct). cpp-gen now emits fields THEN operations in one struct -- pinned by rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/tests/wi931_eponymous_struct_test.rs and, since WI-935, by lf1_test::lf1_vec3_and_euler_emit_correctly against the real stdlib Vec3. scaland's Bootstrap has the same band split and has NOT had that fix.\n\nPRE-EXISTING, SURFACED NOW. It is not caused by WI-935. It went unexercised because the tree shipped no stdlib sort of this shape -- WI-931's own fixture comment says so ('the tree currently ships no sort of this shape, and a rule with no example is exactly the one that rots'). WI-935 made Vec3 the first one, which is what exposed it.\n\nWHAT WI-935 DID INSTEAD, so this is not silently encoded as correct: BootstrapTest 'WI-170: standalone entity -> case class' was RE-POINTED from Vec3 to EulerAngles (still a standalone entity, so the test still measures the claim in its name). Vec3's current output is deliberately NOT asserted anywhere -- pinning it would make the bug the contract. scaland core is at its pre-WI-935 baseline (8 failed / 232 passed, all 8 pre-existing: ParseTest:931 plus seven downstream of the cpp_std parse error).\n\nACCEPTANCE: Bootstrap emits ONE declaration for an eponymous single-constructor sort -- a 'case class' carrying the fields, with the operations on it (companion or trait-on-the-same-name, whichever matches scala-forward-mapping) -- driven by a test that generates from the real stdlib geometry and asserts the absence of 'enum Vec3' AND the presence of the case class. A CONTROL that a NON-eponymous multi-constructor sort still emits an enum, and that a plain standalone entity is unchanged. sbt test at or below the current baseline.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-935."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T12:32:57Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-940", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. Bootstrap classifies a sort body into three DISJOINT shapes -- Record (eponymous single constructor) / Sum / Algebra -- and renders each once. `sort Vec3 { entity Vec3(x,y,z); operation vec_add(...) = ... }` now emits `case class Vec3(x: Double, y: Double, z: Double)` where it emitted `enum Vec3: case Vec3(...)`, the nested Vec3.Vec3 that §6.3/WI-926 rule out. Same for TotalFloat, stdlib's other eponymous sort. scaland: 366 passed / 0 failed after `core/clean` (baseline 360 + 6 new). Full rust workspace re-run on the consolidated test layout: 4128 passed, 0 failed.\n\nTHE TICKET'S RECORDED BASELINE WAS STALE: it says 8 failed / 232 passed (ParseTest:931 plus seven downstream of a cpp_std parse error). Measured at claim time: 360 passed, 0 failed. Those were fixed between WI-935 and now.\n\nEPONYMY IS KEYED ON THE ANTHILL NAME, and that is load-bearing rather than pedantic. `Names.scalaTypeName` is MANY-TO-ONE, and stdlib already contains the near-miss: `enum anthill.prelude.Pair { entity pair(fst: A, snd: B) }` -- the constructor is `pair`, the sort `Pair`, and both PascalCase to `Pair`. Comparing the EMITTED names would have collapsed Pair into a case class and broken a delivered test; comparing the written names keeps it a Sum, which is what §6.3 says (\"keyed on the name matching\"). The classifier checks \"any eponymous constructor\" BEFORE \"exactly one constructor\" for the same reason -- reversing those two lines is what makes Pair the wrong shape.\n\nDEFECT (2) OF THE TICKET IS ANSWERED BY A SCALA LIMIT, NOT IMPLEMENTED. The ticket asks for the operations \"on it\", citing cpp-gen putting an eponymous sort's fields AND operations in one `struct` (WI-931). That collapse has NO Scala analogue: a C++ member function DECLARATION needs no definition, while Scala has no abstract member in an instantiable `case class`, and bootstrap emits signatures only (bodies are the KB-driven gen's, proposal 034). So the members stay on the `<Sort>Ops` contract §2.3 already uses for EVERY sort with constructors -- a uniform mapping rule, not a carve-out for eponymous sorts. What §6.3 buys in Scala is that the TYPE is one declaration. docs/kernel-language.md §6.3 said \"the fields and operations belong to one type in every backend\", which over-claimed; it now states that what every backend owes is one declaration OF THE SORT'S OWN NAME, and that a differently-named companion is not the forbidden shape.\n\nBEYOND THE TICKET -- THE INVARIANT IS NOW TOTAL. §6.3 also admits an eponymous variant ALONGSIDE siblings (WI-946), and Scala has no spelling for that at all: the sum and one of its cases would have to be one name in one scope. That is REFUSED (`BootstrapError`, located) rather than emitted as `enum S: case S`, which would be the nested S.S in a second guise. So after this change no Bootstrap path can emit a nested X.X -- a stronger claim than \"the single-constructor case is fixed\". No stdlib file has that shape (measured), which is exactly why it needed a local fixture.\n\nCONTROL DISCIPLINE, MEASURED NOT ASSERTED, both probes run and reverted:\n  * `shapeOf` returning Sum for the eponymous single-ctor case (the old behaviour) fails EXACTLY FOUR tests: the Vec3 one, TotalFloat, the sugar/long-form equivalence, and the parametric `case class Box[T]`.\n  * replacing the sibling refusal with `Sum(ctors)` fails EXACTLY ONE: the REFUSED test. The record probe leaves it green, so the two claims are pinned separately.\n  * `WI-940 CONTROL: a non-eponymous multi-constructor sort still emits an enum` and `WI-170: standalone entity -> case class` pass BOTH ways BY DESIGN, and say so at their sites.\n\nTHE STRONGEST NEW TEST is the §6.3 EQUIVALENCE: `entity Acct(...)` and `sort Acct { entity Acct(...) }` are asserted BYTE-EQUAL as whole files, not by two independent expectations. That is what the spec actually claims (\"a codegen backend naming Acct gets the same answer either way\"), and it holds by CONSTRUCTION now -- `emitStandaloneEntity` and the Record branch call one `renderCaseClass`, where they previously had two independently-written templates that had drifted.\n\nREVIEW: /code-review is user-triggered and cannot be model-invoked, so four finder agents were run instead (reuse, simplification, altitude, test-quality) and all three actionable findings applied: BootstrapError now carries `span: Span` and renders through Span.render, the ONE located renderer ParseError/LoadError already share (it was pre-flattening the location into a bare string); the equivalence test asserts both sides emit one file rather than relying on `.head` happening to throw; `count` -> `exists`. The test-quality agent independently re-derived both control counts by branch-tracing and got the same numbers. THE USER MUST STILL RUN /code-review -- it is the repo's stated pre-commit gate and I cannot invoke it.\n\nFOUND WHILE HERE, FILED NOT FIXED: dumping the real emitted stdlib output for the first time showed Pair.scala does not compile, from two independent mechanisms, both PRE-EXISTING and neither touched by this change. Verified with `scala-cli compile --scala 3.6.3`, not asserted. WI-1021 (a sort's own name rewritten through the prelude type map -> `def fst(p: Tuple2): A`, four \"Missing type parameter\") and WI-1022 (`requires` -> `extends` on the data declaration -> \"trait PartialEq is extended twice\", \"conflicting base types\"). Both hang off WI-1020: §2.3 promises \"The generated file compiles as-is\" and NOTHING verifies it -- every Bootstrap assertion is a substring match, which is precisely how both survived.\n\nNOTE ON NUMBERING: those three were first filed as WI-1011/1012/1013 and renumbered to 1015/1016/1017 on rebase -- upstream had taken 1011-1014 in the interim.", at: "2026-08-05T12:32:56Z")
